{"parentUuid":"1f5a7c73-56a7-4dc4-b3d2-6745b252a262","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf8ravJnKM5Dy3C7Knhrm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now the delta, pipeline, and http lines:"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2184,"cache_read_input_tokens":948072,"output_tokens":874,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2184,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":874,"cache_read_input_tokens":948072,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2184,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2184},"type":"message","model":null}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"requestId":"req_011Cf8rapehjyMxp9Wv8Kfmp","type":"assistant","uuid":"bc044dce-63af-41d2-9c04-ab46bd8e681f","timestamp":"2026-09-17T10:29:51.930Z","effort":"high","session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.231","gitBranch":"fix/audit-correctness-paths","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
{"parentUuid":"bc044dce-63af-41d2-9c04-ab46bd8e681f","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf8ravJnKM5Dy3C7Knhrm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Dfu8KZF9UZLhsbAQbHBZKm","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\npython3 - <<'PY'\n# delta: unit-test the typed classifier (covers the matches! body)\np=\"crates/common/delta/src/connection.rs\"; s=open(p).read()\nadd = '''\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod missing_table_tests {\n    use super::is_missing_table;\n\n    #[test]\n    fn only_a_typed_object_store_not_found_means_no_table_here() {\n        // A genuinely absent table: object-store NotFound.\n        let absent = deltalake::DeltaTableError::ObjectStore {\n            source: deltalake::ObjectStoreError::NotFound {\n                path: \"s3://b/t/_delta_log\".into(),\n                source: \"no such key\".into(),\n            },\n        };\n        assert!(is_missing_table(&absent));\n\n        // The regression this pins (#654 C2): a permission failure whose\n        // rendered text contains \"not found\"-shaped wording must NOT be read\n        // as \"no table here\" — doing so made the sink write a fresh\n        // `_delta_log/…0.json` over an EXISTING table and orphan its data\n        // files. Only the typed variant counts now.\n        let denied = deltalake::DeltaTableError::ObjectStore {\n            source: deltalake::ObjectStoreError::Generic {\n                store: \"S3\",\n                source: \"403 AccessDenied: The specified key does not exist\".into(),\n            },\n        };\n        assert!(\n            !is_missing_table(&denied),\n            \"a 403 must never be mistaken for an absent table\"\n        );\n\n        // An explicit NotATable is still the other legitimate signal.\n        assert!(is_missing_table(&deltalake::DeltaTableError::NotATable(\n            \"no log\".into()\n        )));\n    }\n}\n'''\ns = s.rstrip() + \"\\n\" + add\nopen(p,\"w\").write(s)\nPY\nTOOL=~/.rustup/toolchains/1.96.0-aarch64-apple-darwin/bin\nRUSTC=$TOOL/rustc $TOOL/cargo test -p faucet-common-delta --all-features 2>&1 | grep -E \"^error|test result|FAILED\" -A 6 | head -16","description":"Unit-test the delta typed classifier","timeout":900000},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2184,"cache_read_input_tokens":948072,"output_tokens":874,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2184,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":874,"cache_read_input_tokens":948072,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2184,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2184},"type":"message","model":null}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"requestId":"req_011Cf8rapehjyMxp9Wv8Kfmp","type":"assistant","uuid":"7406b1f0-c82d-4c8a-aaac-e51c5f2a4d4f","timestamp":"2026-09-17T10:30:00.111Z","effort":"high","session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.231","gitBranch":"fix/audit-correctness-paths","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-09-17T10:30:43.595Z","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ab6fa6999782a78d5</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_017a17dYCSjd8vpj1sLnZPEf</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-c-sikawats-Documents-GitHub-Personal-faucet-stream/2e42fef2-d7da-4f6e-97b1-064e451b2570/tasks/ab6fa6999782a78d5.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Dedupe backoff + SQL escaping\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>All four findings are fixed, all gates green, changes left unstaged in the working tree.\n\n## Finding 1 (#654 M13) — four unjittered backoff copies deduped onto core\n\n| File | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| `crates/sink/kinesis/src/sink.rs:33` | local `backoff_delay`, no jitter | keeps configured `initial_ms`/`max_ms` exponential, delegates jitter to `faucet_core::retry::apply_jitter` |\n| `crates/sink/sqs/src/sink.rs:31` | byte-identical copy | same |\n| `crates/source/kinesis/src/shard.rs:22,30` | `backoff_delay` + `jitter_fraction` (fixed 0–25% per-shard hash) | `jitter_fraction` **deleted**; `backoff_delay` caps at a named `MAX_BACKOFF` (30s) then `apply_jitter`. The old fraction was constant per shard, so after one shared throttle all shards still retried inside the same narrow window in fixed order — it never broke a herd |\n| `crates/source/grpc/src/stream.rs:786` | `next_backoff(current, cap)` doubler + `mut backoff` state | `next_backoff` **deleted**, replaced by pure `reconnect_delay(base, cap, attempt)`; the `mut backoff` variable is gone (derived from the existing `attempt` counter, so the two can no longer drift). Honours the configured `reconnect_max_backoff`, not core's 60s |\n\nConfigured base/cap semantics preserved everywhere — `apply_jitter` is used (not `backoff_with_jitter`) precisely so each connector's own ceiling wins. Tests: each crate's exact-equality backoff test became a `[0.5, 1.5)`-band assertion (incl. \"never zero\" and \"configured cap, not core's\"), plus a new `*_is_decorrelated_across_calls` test proving repeated identical inputs do not return an identical delay.\n\n## Finding 2 (#654 M6) — WebSocket reconnect now grows and is bounded\nNew pure `reconnect_delay(base, attempt)` in `crates/source/websocket/src/stream.rs` routes through `backoff_with_jitter` (base = the unchanged `reconnect_backoff` config field, which supplies the growth base; core supplies the ceiling the config lacks). Both sleep sites (connect-failure `:236`, reconnect `:378`) now compute from the existing `reconnect_attempts` counter and log the real delay. Tests: growth across attempts 0–4 within band, boundedness at attempts 30/1000/`usize::MAX` (`&gt;=30s` and `&lt;90s`), and decorrelation.\n\n## Finding 3 (#654 M14) — one ANSI escaper, one justified dialect divergence\n`faucet_core::write_mode::sql_literal` is now `pub` + re-exported from `lib.rs`, with a doc comment stating it is the ANSI escaper and that a dialect needing other rules must say so explicitly. Two new core tests cover every scalar kind, empty string, multiple quotes, non-scalars→`NULL`, and a breakout attempt.\n\n**Deliberate divergence kept:** ClickHouse must keep its own escaper. I verified against ClickHouse's syntax reference — backslash *is* a meta-character in its string literals (\"you need to escape at least `'` and `\\`\"), so ANSI quote-doubling alone is unsafe there: a value ending in `\\` emits `'foo\\'`, whose backslash eats the closing quote. `faucet_common_clickhouse::sql_literal` keeps `\\\\`/`\\'`, now with a `# Why not faucet_core::sql_literal` section naming both failure modes, plus a new test asserting exactly the inputs ANSI would mishandle (`foo\\`, `a\\'`, `x\\' OR 1=1 --`) and a property check that no backslash is left dangling.\n\n## Finding 4 (#654 M14, second-order injection) — single non-re-scanning pass\n`substitute_context_sql` no longer loops `String::replace` per context entry over its own output. Values are pre-rendered to ClickHouse literals and handed to core's audited single left-to-right scanner (`faucet_core::util::substitute_context`), which never re-scans replacement text — so no duplicated scanner logic.\n\n**I also fixed the same defect across the two substitution *kinds*.** `build_effective_query` previously substituted context first, then `@bookmark` over the result — so a context value containing `@bookmark` picked up the cursor literal and broke out of its own quotes. `@bookmark` is now normalised to a reserved `{__faucet_bookmark}` key (named consts `BOOKMARK_TOKEN`/`BOOKMARK_KEY`) on the *raw author-controlled template*, and everything is substituted in one pass. Reserved key shadows any same-named context entry.\n\n**On bind parameters** (you asked me to evaluate first): I chose escaping. ClickHouse's parameter syntax is `{name:Type}` — it requires an explicit ClickHouse type inside the placeholder. Routing the documented `{key}` grammar through it would change the user-facing token syntax *and* force per-JSON-value ClickHouse type inference (int vs float, date-like strings), i.e. a breaking config change carrying fresh mis-typing bugs. Rationale is recorded in the function's doc comment.\n\nSix new tests: no re-substitution of a value naming another key; neither direction of context↔bookmark injection; order-independence (same context built forward and reversed yields byte-identical SQL); reserved key cannot be shadowed; unmatched placeholders left verbatim.\n\n## Verification\n`cargo fmt` clean on all 9 files. Tests green: core 1170, sink-kinesis 17, sink-sqs 13, source-kinesis 19, source-grpc 22, common-clickhouse 21, source-clickhouse 34, source-websocket 27. `clippy --all-targets --all-features -D warnings` clean on all 8 crates. Index is empty; all changes are unstaged working-tree edits. No files outside my list were modified.\n\n## Two things you need to action\n\n1. **A peer agent lost work and I could not safely restore it.** I ran `git stash` to get a clean baseline for a pre-existing-failure check — a mistake with parallel agents, since it reverted *everyone's* uncommitted work. I recovered 24 files from the stash (mine and peers'). For the other 12, peers had already re-edited them, so I left their live versions alone. Eleven of those twelve lost nothing, but **`crates/source/postgres-cdc/src/replication.rs` has 95 lines in the stash that are absent from the current file** (stash: +103/−31; current: +1/−1, a newer edit not in the stash — the two diverge, so I did not merge). The stash entry **`stash@{0}` is intentionally retained**; that agent can recover with `git checkout stash@{0} -- crates/source/postgres-cdc/src/replication.rs` (which would drop its newer one-line `slot_in_use()` change) or diff against the copy I saved at `/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-c-sikawats-Documents-GitHub-Personal-faucet-stream/55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f/scratchpad/replication.rs.stashed`. Please don't drop that stash until they've looked.\n\n2. **A docs-sync follow-up outside my file list.** The WebSocket `reconnect_backoff` semantics changed from constant to exponential base, so three places now read wrong and I was scoped out of editing them: `crates/source/websocket/src/config.rs:70` (\"Fixed wait (seconds) between reconnect attempts\" — this doc comment becomes `faucet schema` output), and `crates/source/websocket/README.md` lines 16, 87, and 296 (\"fixed `reconnect_backoff`\" / \"Fixed wait between reconnect attempts\" / \"waits `reconnect_backoff`\").\n\nAlso worth knowing: the shared disk hit 0 bytes mid-run and corrupted build artifacts for every agent. I reclaimed ~1GB of `target/**/incremental` (sanctioned as safe in the maintenance rules) and ran `cargo clean -p` on the 8 packages reporting corrupted rlibs/build scripts; disk is now ~60Gi free. The `conformance_capabilities_truthful` test failure in `faucet-sink-kinesis` and `faucet-sink-sqs` is **pre-existing** — I confirmed it fails at HEAD with my changes stashed, and another agent is editing those crates' `config.rs` and `README.md`.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>417322</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>474</tool_uses><duration_ms>4504820</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
