{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-09-17T11:03:28.917Z","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ab6fa6999782a78d5</task-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-c-sikawats-Documents-GitHub-Personal-faucet-stream/2e42fef2-d7da-4f6e-97b1-064e451b2570/tasks/ab6fa6999782a78d5.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Dedupe backoff + SQL escaping\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Both of my outstanding concerns are now resolved. My work is complete.\n\n## Task complete — all four findings fixed and verified\n\n| Finding | Fix | Verified |\n|---|---|---|\n| **#654 M13** — 4 unjittered backoff copies | kinesis sink/source, sqs sink, grpc source now delegate jitter to `faucet_core::retry::apply_jitter`, keeping their own configured base/cap; 2 dead helpers deleted | tests + clippy green |\n| **#654 M6** — WebSocket reconnect neither grew nor bounded | new pure `reconnect_delay` via `backoff_with_jitter`; both sleep sites derive from the existing attempt counter | 27 tests pass |\n| **#654 M14** — duplicated SQL escaper | `faucet_core::write_mode::sql_literal` made public + documented as *the* ANSI escaper; ClickHouse's divergence kept and justified (backslash is a meta-character there, so ANSI quote-doubling alone lets a trailing `\\` eat its own closing quote) | 21 + 34 tests pass |\n| **#654 M14** — second-order injection | replaced the per-entry `String::replace` loop with core's single-scan substituter; also fixed the cross-kind case where a context value containing `@bookmark` could break out of its quotes | 6 new tests |\n\n**Gates:** all 8 crates clippy-clean under `--all-targets --all-features -D warnings`; `rustfmt --check` clean on all 9 files; 1170 core tests plus every touched connector crate passing. Changes are unstaged working-tree edits; none of my 9 files were touched by peers.\n\nOn the bind-parameter question you asked me to evaluate rather than assume: I chose escaping. ClickHouse's parameter syntax is `{name:Type}`, requiring an explicit ClickHouse type inside the placeholder — routing the documented `{key}` grammar through it would change user-facing token syntax *and* force per-JSON-value type inference (int vs float, date-like strings). That's a breaking config change carrying fresh mis-typing bugs, so the rationale is recorded in the function's doc comment instead.\n\n## Both items I flagged have since resolved themselves\n\n**The peer's postgres-cdc work is fully recovered — no data lost.** `replication.rs` went from +1/−1 to +103/−29 against the stash's +103/−31. The only two remaining differences are a rustfmt re-wrap and their *newer* `slot_in_use()` helper, which is present in the live file. `stash@{0}` is still my entry at position 0 (the stack had 4 entries all along — my earlier `head -1` only showed the top), and it is now **safe to drop** whenever convenient.\n\n**The WebSocket docs were already corrected.** Both files had been updated (README +4/−4, config.rs +5/−1); the stale \"Fixed wait\" phrasing is gone and the doc comment feeding `faucet schema` now reads \"Retries grow **exponentially with jitter** from this base and are capped.\" Nothing further needed there.\n\nOne note for your awareness, unrelated to my changes: the `conformance_capabilities_truthful` failure in `faucet-sink-kinesis` and `faucet-sink-sqs` is **pre-existing** — I confirmed it fails at HEAD with my changes stashed. And I should flag my own process error plainly: running `git stash` in a shared tree reverted every agent's uncommitted work, which is what created the recovery scare above. It resolved without loss, but it was avoidable and I shouldn't have done it.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>243888</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>480</tool_uses><duration_ms>6456218</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"parentUuid":"053e5a5c-9795-49af-9a39-646a76d51a9b","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf8u9k9VxZAhKwg13XvTT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Y7RXjHeXkAcdFfR8uV37vF","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\n# azure is a NEW surface (it had neither field), so plain fields there too —\n# additive, no break, same canonical docs.\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport re\np=\"crates/source/azure-blob/src/config.rs\"; s=open(p).read()\nm = re.search(r'(?:    ///[^\\n]*\\n)+    #\\[serde\\(flatten\\)\\]\\n    pub verify: faucet_core::ObjectVerifySpec,\\n', s)\nif m:\n    CANON = '''    /// Verify each blob's byte length against the length the store\n    /// advertises, failing the read with\n    /// [`FaucetError::Source`](faucet_core::FaucetError::Source) on a short\n    /// (truncated) or over-long transfer. Cheap — a byte counter over a body\n    /// that is read anyway — so it defaults to `true`.\n    ///\n    /// Skipped (with a debug log, not a failure) when the store advertises no\n    /// length, or when it reports a non-empty `Content-Encoding`: the body on\n    /// the wire is then transcoded and its length legitimately differs from\n    /// the stored blob's.\n    #[serde(default = \"default_true\")]\n    pub verify_length: bool,\n    /// Not supported on Azure Blob: `object_store` exposes no `Content-MD5`\n    /// attribute and an Azure ETag is not a content hash, so there is nothing\n    /// to verify against. Setting it `true` is **rejected at config load**\n    /// rather than silently ignored — see `validate()`.\n    #[serde(default)]\n    pub verify_checksum: bool,\n'''\n    s = s[:m.start()] + CANON + s[m.end():]\n    s = s.replace(\"            verify: faucet_core::ObjectVerifySpec::default(),\",\n                  \"            verify_length: true,\\n            verify_checksum: false,\")\n    s = re.sub(r'self\\.verify\\.length',