{"parentUuid":"c91d6920-1275-406c-96d1-2b22c21e0578","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf99867rr5n5x2JDmAbTn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`NodeReport` is `#[non_exhaustive]`, so a typed field is additive. Filing that one too:"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":420,"cache_read_input_tokens":283298,"output_tokens":2706,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":420,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2706,"cache_read_input_tokens":283298,"cache_creation_input_tokens":420,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":420},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cf9985RRum6MdxvRqcknc","type":"assistant","uuid":"ea9f4227-4bd8-49ce-84d0-256d5ce9c2b3","timestamp":"2026-09-17T14:06:43.267Z","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"fix/audit-correctness-paths","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
{"parentUuid":"ea9f4227-4bd8-49ce-84d0-256d5ce9c2b3","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf99867rr5n5x2JDmAbTn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Fa9UPrKA7i5uv61CY9Vg3m","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\ngh auth switch --user PawanSikawat >/dev/null 2>&1 && gh issue create \\\n  --title \"enhancement(core/cli): carry a typed failure kind on \\`NodeReport\\` so topology runs get the same circuit-open cooldown as matrix runs\" \\\n  --label enhancement --label tier-2 \\\n  --body \"$(cat <<'BODY'\n## Summary\n\n`faucet_core::topology::NodeReport::error` is an `Option<String>` — the node's\nfailure is rendered to text before it leaves the executor. Everything\ndownstream that wants to react to a *kind* of failure therefore cannot, and the\nconcrete consequence today is that **a topology run whose node tripped the\nresilience circuit breaker does not get the scheduler's cooldown**, while the\nidentical failure in matrix mode does.\n\nMatrix mode was just fixed for exactly this (#654 M2): `InvocationOutcome` now\ncarries `executor::InvocationErrorKind`, and `schedule::classify` keys the\ncooldown off `InvocationErrorKind::CircuitOpen` instead of the `CircuitOpen`\ndisplay prefix. `cli/src/topology.rs::run_topology` has to set\n`error_kind: None` for every node outcome, with a comment saying why:\n\n```rust\nerror: n.error.clone(),\n// A topology node reports its failure as an already-rendered\n// string (`NodeReport.error`), so there is no typed error left to\n// classify here.\nerror_kind: None,\n```\n\nSo the fix stopped one layer short of the graph runtime.\n\n## Motivation\n\nPrimary Goal — reliability. The cooldown exists so a cron tick does not\nre-fire immediately at a destination that just tripped its own breaker; losing\nit in topology mode means the pipeline hammers a failing endpoint on every\ntick, which is the behaviour the breaker was added to prevent.\n\nIt is also the same PRINCIPLES §6 violation one level down: the typed\n`FaucetError::CircuitOpen` **is** available inside the node task, and is\ndiscarded at the reporting boundary. Fixing it removes the last place in the\nrun path where a failure class has to be recovered from prose (or, as now,\ncannot be recovered at all).\n\n## Proposed design\n\n`NodeReport` is already `#[non_exhaustive]`, so adding a field is a MINOR bump\nunder the declared semver contract — no major, no deprecation.\n\n1. **core** — add a typed kind alongside the rendered message:\n   ```rust\n   /// Typed class of `error`, for callers that react to a kind of failure\n   /// rather than report it. `None` when the node succeeded.\n   pub error_kind: Option<NodeErrorKind>,\n   ```\n   with\n   ```rust\n   #[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\n   #[non_exhaustive]\n   pub enum NodeErrorKind { CircuitOpen, Other }\n   ```\n   Classify it where the node's `Result<_, FaucetError>` is still intact, in\n   the per-node task join in `run_reported`, via one pure\n   `fn classify(&FaucetError) -> NodeErrorKind`. Keep `error` exactly as it is\n   — this is additive, and the message is what users read.\n\n   Consider putting the classifier next to `FaucetError` instead and having\n   both `NodeErrorKind` and the CLI's `InvocationErrorKind` derive from it, so\n   there is one table rather than two that can drift. That is the better shape\n   if the two enums stay in step; if they diverge (the CLI's covers\n   `CliError`, not just `FaucetError`) keep them separate and cross-reference.\n\n2. **cli** — in `run_topology`, map `NodeErrorKind` → `InvocationErrorKind`\n   when building each node's `InvocationOutcome`, replacing the\n   `error_kind: None` and its comment. The scheduler then needs no change at\n   all: `classify` already reads the field.\n\n3. Extend `post_run_observability` if a per-node kind is worth a metric label\n   — but only if it does not raise cardinality (`kind` is a closed set, so it\n   is safe).\n\n### Affected crates / files\n\n- `crates/core/src/topology.rs` — `NodeReport`, the new enum, the classifier,\n  and `run_reported`'s join arm.\n- `cli/src/topology.rs` — `run_topology`'s outcome construction.\n- `cli/src/executor.rs` — possibly a `From<NodeErrorKind>` for\n  `InvocationErrorKind`.\n- `.claude/rules/architecture.md` — the `topology.rs` \"Reporting (#459)\"\n  paragraph describing `NodeReport`.\n\n## Edge cases & failure modes\n\n- **A node cancelled by `TopologyOnError::Propagate`** must not be classified\n  `CircuitOpen` just because a *sibling* tripped — only the node that raised\n  it. Pin this with a test: one node trips, a sibling is cancelled, exactly\n  one report carries `CircuitOpen`.\n- **A panicked node task** has no typed error; `error_kind` stays `None`\n  (distinct from `Some(Other)`), matching the convention the CLI's\n  `InvocationErrorKind` doc already states.\n- **Multiple nodes trip** — the scheduler applies one cooldown; `classify`\n  already uses `.any(..)`, so no change, but worth a test.\n- **`FaucetError::Custom`** wrapping a third-party error that is semantically\n  a breaker trip cannot be classified. That is correct and should stay so —\n  do not sniff inside `Custom`.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] `NodeReport` carries `error_kind`, classified from the typed\n      `FaucetError` inside the node task.\n- [ ] A topology run whose node raises `FaucetError::CircuitOpen` under\n      `faucet schedule` delays the next tick by the policy cooldown, asserted\n      by a test that goes through `classify` (mirroring\n      `classify_recovers_cooldown_from_circuit_open_invocation`).\n- [ ] A reworded `CircuitOpen` display string cannot change the outcome, and a\n      lookalike message in another variant does not earn a cooldown — the same\n      two-sided test matrix matrix mode now has.\n- [ ] A cancelled sibling is not misclassified.\n- [ ] `cargo semver-checks` reports \"no semver update required\" beyond a minor\n      for `faucet-core`.\n- [ ] `.claude/rules/architecture.md` updated.\n- [ ] ≥95% patch coverage.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Replacing `NodeReport.error` with a typed error object — the rendered\n  message is what the CLI prints and the HTTP API returns; this issue only\n  adds a classification beside it.\n- Widening `NodeErrorKind` beyond `CircuitOpen` / `Other` speculatively. Add a\n  variant when a consumer needs to distinguish one.\n- Any change to `TopologyResult`, which is exhaustively constructible and\n  therefore frozen.\n\n## References\n\n- #654 (principles audit) — M2 fixed the matrix path; this is the graph path\n  the same finding left behind.\n- PR that fixed M2: the `InvocationErrorKind` + `executor::classify_error`\n  commit on `fix/audit-correctness-paths`.\n- `crates/core/src/topology.rs` — `NodeReport` is already `#[non_exhaustive]`\n  precisely so a per-node field can be added in a minor release.\n- Roadmap epic #38.\nBODY\n)\" 2>&1 | tail -3","description":"File the typed NodeReport issue"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":420,"cache_read_input_tokens":283298,"output_tokens":2706,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":420,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2706,"cache_read_input_tokens":283298,"cache_creation_input_tokens":420,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":420},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Fa9UPrKA7i5uv61CY9Vg3m":{"command":"cd /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\ngh auth switch --user PawanSikawat >/dev/null 2>&1 && gh issue create \\\n  --title \"enhancement(core/cli): carry a typed failure kind on \\`NodeReport\\` so topology runs get the same circuit-open cooldown as matrix runs\" \\\n  --label enhancement --label tier-2 \\\n  --body \"$(cat <<'BODY'\n## Summary\n\n`faucet_core::topology::NodeReport::error` is an `Option<String>` — the node's\nfailure is rendered to text before it leaves the executor. Everything\ndownstream that wants to react to a *kind* of failure therefore cannot, and the\nconcrete consequence today is that **a topology run whose node tripped the\nresilience circuit breaker does not get the scheduler's cooldown**, while the\nidentical failure in matrix mode does.\n\nMatrix mode was just fixed for exactly this (#654 M2): `InvocationOutcome` now\ncarries `executor::InvocationErrorKind`, and `schedule::classify` keys the\ncooldown off `InvocationErrorKind::CircuitOpen` instead of the `CircuitOpen`\ndisplay prefix. `cli/src/topology.rs::run_topology` has to set\n`error_kind: None` for every node outcome, with a comment saying why:\n\n```rust\nerror: n.error.clone(),\n// A topology node reports its failure as an already-rendered\n// string (`NodeReport.error`), so there is no typed error left to\n// classify here.\nerror_kind: None,\n```\n\nSo the fix stopped one layer short of the graph runtime.\n\n## Motivation\n\nPrimary Goal — reliability. The cooldown exists so a cron tick does not\nre-fire immediately at a destination that just tripped its own breaker; losing\nit in topology mode means the pipeline hammers a failing endpoint on every\ntick, which is the behaviour the breaker was added to prevent.\n\nIt is also the same PRINCIPLES §6 violation one level down: the typed\n`FaucetError::CircuitOpen` **is** available inside the node task, and is\ndiscarded at the reporting boundary. Fixing it removes the last place in the\nrun path where a failure class has to be recovered from prose (or, as now,\ncannot be recovered at all).\n\n## Proposed design\n\n`NodeReport` is already `#[non_exhaustive]`, so adding a field is a MINOR bump\nunder the declared semver contract — no major, no deprecation.\n\n1. **core** — add a typed kind alongside the rendered message:\n   ```rust\n   /// Typed class of `error`, for callers that react to a kind of failure\n   /// rather than report it. `None` when the node succeeded.\n   pub error_kind: Option<NodeErrorKind>,\n   ```\n   with\n   ```rust\n   #[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\n   #[non_exhaustive]\n   pub enum NodeErrorKind { CircuitOpen, Other }\n   ```\n   Classify it where the node's `Result<_, FaucetError>` is still intact, in\n   the per-node task join in `run_reported`, via one pure\n   `fn classify(&FaucetError) -> NodeErrorKind`. Keep `error` exactly as it is\n   — this is additive, and the message is what users read.\n\n   Consider putting the classifier next to `FaucetError` instead and having\n   both `NodeErrorKind` and the CLI's `InvocationErrorKind` derive from it, so\n   there is one table rather than two that can drift. That is the better shape\n   if the two enums stay in step; if they diverge (the CLI's covers\n   `CliError`, not just `FaucetError`) keep them separate and cross-reference.\n\n2. **cli** — in `run_topology`, map `NodeErrorKind` → `InvocationErrorKind`\n   when building each node's `InvocationOutcome`, replacing the\n   `error_kind: None` and its comment. The scheduler then needs no change at\n   all: `classify` already reads the field.\n\n3. Extend `post_run_observability` if a per-node kind is worth a metric label\n   — but only if it does not raise cardinality (`kind` is a closed set, so it\n   is safe).\n\n### Affected crates / files\n\n- `crates/core/src/topology.rs` — `NodeReport`, the new enum, the classifier,\n  and `run_reported`'s join arm.\n- `cli/src/topology.rs` — `run_topology`'s outcome construction.\n- `cli/src/executor.rs` — possibly a `From<NodeErrorKind>` for\n  `InvocationErrorKind`.\n- `.claude/rules/architecture.md` — the `topology.rs` \"Reporting (#459)\"\n  paragraph describing `NodeReport`.\n\n## Edge cases & failure modes\n\n- **A node cancelled by `TopologyOnError::Propagate`** must not be classified\n  `CircuitOpen` just because a *sibling* tripped — only the node that raised\n  it. Pin this with a test: one node trips, a sibling is cancelled, exactly\n  one report carries `CircuitOpen`.\n- **A panicked node task** has no typed error; `error_kind` stays `None`\n  (distinct from `Some(Other)`), matching the convention the CLI's\n  `InvocationErrorKind` doc already states.\n- **Multiple nodes trip** — the scheduler applies one cooldown; `classify`\n  already uses `.any(..)`, so no change, but worth a test.\n- **`FaucetError::Custom`** wrapping a third-party error that is semantically\n  a breaker trip cannot be classified. That is correct and should stay so —\n  do not sniff inside `Custom`.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] `NodeReport` carries `error_kind`, classified from the typed\n      `FaucetError` inside the node task.\n- [ ] A topology run whose node raises `FaucetError::CircuitOpen` under\n      `faucet schedule` delays the next tick by the policy cooldown, asserted\n      by a test that goes through `classify` (mirroring\n      `classify_recovers_cooldown_from_circuit_open_invocation`).\n- [ ] A reworded `CircuitOpen` display string cannot change the outcome, and a\n      lookalike message in another variant does not earn a cooldown — the same\n      two-sided test matrix matrix mode now has.\n- [ ] A cancelled sibling is not misclassified.\n- [ ] `cargo semver-checks` reports \"no semver update required\" beyond a minor\n      for `faucet-core`.\n- [ ] `.claude/rules/architecture.md` updated.\n- [ ] ≥95% patch coverage.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Replacing `NodeReport.error` with a typed error object — the rendered\n  message is what the CLI prints and the HTTP API returns; this issue only\n  adds a classification beside it.\n- Widening `NodeErrorKind` beyond `CircuitOpen` / `Other` speculatively. Add a\n  variant when a consumer needs to distinguish one.\n- Any change to `TopologyResult`, which is exhaustively constructible and\n  therefore frozen.\n\n## References\n\n- #654 (principles audit) — M2 fixed the matrix path; this is the graph path\n  the same finding left behind.\n- PR that fixed M2: the `InvocationErrorKind` + `executor::classify_error`\n  commit on `fix/audit-correctness-paths`.\n- `crates/core/src/topology.rs` — `NodeReport` is already `#[non_exhaustive]`\n  precisely so a per-node field can be added in a minor release.\n- Roadmap epic #38.\nBODY\n)\" 2>&1 | tail -3","description":"File the typed NodeReport issue"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cf9985RRum6MdxvRqcknc","type":"assistant","uuid":"07e37a18-a99e-4276-8209-ae2a13e7fa52","timestamp":"2026-09-17T14:07:17.280Z","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"fix/audit-correctness-paths","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
